# Alerts

Nocturne can tell you, or someone who looks out for you, when something needs
attention: glucose going too low or too high, falling fast, the sensor going
quiet, the loop stopping, a pump site getting old. You decide what counts as
"needs attention" by writing **alert rules**.

This section explains how rules work, what every condition means, and how to
combine them, with examples you can run on this page.

<Callout type="warning" title="Keep your device alarms switched on">

  Nocturne's alerts are an extra layer, not a replacement for the alarms built
  into your CGM app, pump or loop app. A Nocturne alert can only fire when
  readings reach your Nocturne site, and it can only reach you when the way it
  is delivered (your phone, a chat app, email) is working. Phones lose signal,
  apps get closed, servers restart. Keep the alarms on the devices themselves
  switched on.

</Callout>

<Callout type="info" title="Not medical advice">

  The numbers on these pages (70 mg/dL, 15 minutes and so on) show how the
  settings behave. They are not recommendations. Decide your own thresholds
  with your diabetes care team.

</Callout>

## How an alert works

Every alert comes from a rule, and every rule has three parts:

1. **A condition**: what Nocturne watches for, such as "glucose below 70
   mg/dL". A condition can be one check or several combined.
2. **Channels**: where the alert goes, such as a notification in your
   browser, a message in Discord or Telegram, or an email.
3. **Settings for afterwards**: how urgent the alert is, whether it can
   switch itself off, and what snoozing it does.

Nocturne checks your rules every time a new reading arrives, which for most
CGMs is every 5 minutes. Rules about time passing, like "no reading for 30
minutes", are also checked every 30 seconds, because nothing arrives to trigger
them.

When a rule's condition becomes true, the alert **fires**: it is recorded,
and it is sent to every channel on the rule. It stays open until the
condition stops being true, it is closed another way (see
[When an alert fires](/docs/alerts/when-an-alert-fires)), or the rule is
switched off.

Try it. Below is a rule that alerts when glucose is below 70 mg/dL (3.9
mmol/L), run against an evening where glucose drifts down after midnight. Move
across the graph, or scrub the bar under it, to see what the rule saw at each
moment.

<AlertSimulator scenario="overnight-low" rules={["low"]} />

The marker on the graph is where the alert fired: the first reading below 70.
The light beside the rule is on for as long as the rule is true, and goes out
with the first reading back above 70.

## The Alerts page

Rules live under **Alerts** in the sidebar. The page lists every rule on your
site, with a switch to turn each one off and a button to send a test alert.
The tiles at the top count your active rules, the alerts sounding right now,
and how many fired this week.

<Screenshot id="alerts-configuration" />

A new site may start with no rules at all. Press **New rule** to write one, or
see [Example rules](/docs/alerts/recipes) for common starting points.

